<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!-- THE PROMISE: this app reads, and only reads.

         Finance is the first module in the suite whose whole job is to talk to somebody else's
         server about this household, so the promise cannot be the one Maintenance and Repository
         make ("nothing identifying leaves the device"). It is a different promise, and a narrower
         one, and it is worth stating exactly:

           1. NOTHING LEAVES THAT YOU DID NOT HAND OVER. The only credentials this app holds are the
              ones typed into Connections — your own Plaid client id and secret, your own Mercury
              API token. It ships with none, it discovers none, and it talks to exactly two hosts:
              the Plaid API and the Mercury API (`logic/Endpoints` is the allow-list, and it is a
              tested function rather than a habit). No analytics, no crash reporter, no "anonymous"
              telemetry. Your balances are not sent anywhere by this module, including to us.

           2. IT CANNOT MOVE MONEY. Every request in `data/net/` is a GET, or the one POST Plaid
              requires to hand back data it already holds. There is no transfer, no payment, no bill
              pay and no ACH call in this module — not disabled, not gated behind a setting: absent.
              Plaid tokens are requested for the read-only products only (`transactions`, `auth`,
              `liabilities`), and Mercury issues genuinely read-only tokens, which is the one the
              Connections screen tells you to make.

           3. THE CREDENTIALS ARE KEPT PROPERLY. Access tokens and API secrets live in
              EncryptedSharedPreferences under a keystore key, never in the database and never in
              the "which tab were you on" preferences file — which is also why the backup
              contributor leaves them behind rather than writing them into a zip that ends up in
              Google Drive. Restore a backup onto a new phone and you reconnect; that is the
              intended cost.

         INTERNET is therefore a permission this app genuinely needs and the others mostly avoid,
         and the answer to "could this request tell anyone something about this household" is, for
         once, yes — it is asking your bank about your money on your behalf. What keeps that honest
         is that the two people it can ask are the two you chose, and it happens because you pressed
         Refresh or because the app came to the foreground with a stale picture. Nothing here polls
         on a timer, wakes the device, or runs when you have not opened the app. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!-- Finance is a library module: no app-level attributes here (name/icon/theme/label live on
         the :app host). Only Finance's own components are declared for the manifest merger. -->
    <application>

        <!-- Not a launcher: the Operations Sandbox home opens this explicitly. -->
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Finance"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize" />

    </application>

</manifest>
